        "width": 568,
        "node_type": "markdown_note",
        "name": "note_1",
        "markdown": "The **LLM Code** block is where Go code is generated from your instruction/prompt.\n\n##### How it works in this recipe:\n\n- You provide a clear prompt (often using Jinja references from earlier columns)\n- The model returns a response\n- The block extracts code content directly for the output column\n\n##### Current status:\n\n- Generated Go is checked by the **Custom check** block with `go vet` + `go build`\n- Rows that fail the checks are filtered out of the final dataset\n\n##### Tip:\n\n- Ask for one self-contained `main.go` with `package main`\n- Ask for explicit error handling\n- Ask for no markdown fences if you want cleaner extraction\n",
        "note_color": "#FEF3C7",
        "note_opacity": "35"
      },
      {
        "id": "note_2",
        "x": 2597.376821192053,
        "y": 1233.2039735099338,
        "width": 471,
        "node_type": "markdown_note",
        "name": "note_2",
        "markdown": "The **Custom check** block runs a tool command over each generated code cell.\n\n##### How this recipe is set up:\n\n- The command is `go vet ./... && go build ./...`\n- Each cell is written to a temp folder with a generated `go.mod` before the command runs\n- Rows whose code fails `go vet` or `go build` are dropped from the final dataset\n- The `go_check` column itself is kept out of the final output (drop is on)\n\n##### Customizing:\n\n- Open the check and edit the **Tool command** or **File extension**\n- Use `{file}` for the generated source file and `{dir}` for its temp folder\n- Examples cover Go (`go vet ./... && go build ./...`), Rust (`cargo check`), and Postgres-flavored SQL (`sqlfluff`)\n\n##### Note:\n\n- The command runs locally, so `go` must be installed on this machine\n- New checks require a one-time consent toggle before a run\n",
        "note_color": "#FEF3C7",
        "note_opacity": "35"
      },
